WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.380
- I wrangled 20 fourth-graders

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.170
at a bowling alley for two hours.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.233
I can literally do anything.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.410
You really find out who your friends are

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.610
when you declare a national emergency.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.443
It's true.

00:00:12.443 --> 00:00:13.440
It's the lesson that Donald Trump

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.270
is learning right about now.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.900
Trump, you'll remember,

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.940
maybe you won't remember,
but you will now,

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.170
declared a national emergency

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:23.110
on the United States' southern
border on February 15th,

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:26.380
and said Trump of his
emergency declaration, quote--

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.343
- It's been signed many times before.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.530
It's been signed by other presidents.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:36.963
From 1977 or so, it gave
the presidents the power.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.130
There's rarely been a problem.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.600
They sign it, nobody cares.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.457
I guess they weren't very exciting.

00:00:44.457 --> 00:00:47.790
- (laughing) And Trump is sort of right.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.210
Presidents have been
declaring national emergencies

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.820
since there, well, since
there have been presidents.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:53.940
Franklin Delano Roosevelt

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.250
declared a national emergency in 1933

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.810
that allowed him to close
all the banks in the country

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.630
on a single day to avoid
them going bankrupt

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.470
in the heart of the Great Depression.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.890
Harry Truman declared a
national emergency in 1950

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.700
to deal with the conflict in Korea.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.670
And in 1970, Richard Nixon
declared an emergency

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.810
in the face of a postal strike,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.450
which allowed him to
assign National Guardsmen

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.850
to deliver the mail, which
is an underrated story

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:20.490
that I would like to read a book about.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:21.560
By the mid-1970s,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.800
there was all sorts of
confusion and controversy

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.810
over when a president could
declare a national emergency

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.420
and what special powers
doing so gave to him.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.140
Enter Congress.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.950
Sometimes fixes problems, I
guess, way back in the past.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.740
This time, they pass the
National Emergencies Act in 1976

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.420
to essential codify what a
president can and should do

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.430
in the face of an urgent
crisis in which they need

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.150
all of the tools of the federal government

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.310
at their disposal.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.450
So, since 1976, presidents have used

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.590
the national emergency
declaration relatively sparingly.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:56.280
Jimmy Carter only issued
one national emergency.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.410
He did it to deal with Americans

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.730
that were being kept hostage in Iran.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.520
Now, his successor, Ronald Reagan,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.060
declared six national emergencies.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.270
George Herbert Walker Bush, only four.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.610
Bill Clinton holds the record

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.380
with 17 national emergency declarations

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.220
during his eight years in the White House.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:13.800
George W. Bush, Barack Obama

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.540
each racked up about 12
emergency declarations each.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.300
Now, the vast majority of all of these

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.730
national emergency declarations

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.360
were in service of
putting sanctions in place

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.080
against bad actors across the world,

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.060
whether that was Lebanon, Syria,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.020
the Congo, or a whole
bunch of other places.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:30.853
And the one thing

00:02:30.853 --> 00:02:33.780
all of those national
emergencies had in common

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.630
was that they had broad
bipartisan support in Congress.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.780
Now, whether they were in
response to some urgent need

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.820
or were part of a
broader pressure campaign

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.800
being brought to bear against a country,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.810
these measures rarely even
drew the attention of Congress

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.960
or much if any news coverage.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.420
But Trump's decision to
declare a state of emergency

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.480
on the Southern border is
then rightly understood

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.090
as the major exception to that rule.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.080
It came after months of back-and-forth

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:00.130
with congressional Democrats

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.820
over how much money, if
any, they would approve

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.090
so that Trump could build his wall

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.570
on the country's southern border.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:11.070
Now, when talks between
the two sides broke down,

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.430
we got a 35-day-long government shutdown,

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.790
the longest in American history.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.560
But even when the government reopened,

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.680
there was no border wall
funding deal to be had.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.840
So, rather than shut down
the government again,

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.760
Trump decided to declare
a national emergency,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.080
which allows him to take money

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.600
Congress has already
approved for other purposes

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.700
and direct it to his wall

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.700
and its first-phase
$5-billion, billion, price tag.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.910
Now, Congress, as you might
guess, didn't love that idea,

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.600
since deciding how the federal
government spends its money

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.800
is the legislature's business.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.410
It's right here, there's
a Constitution back there,

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:49.920
but it's in there, trust me.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.250
So, the Democratic House majority

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.110
introduced a resolution to reverse

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:55.500
the national emergency declaration.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.700
- Well, we are not going
to give any president,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.910
Democratic or Republican, a blank check

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:04.223
to shred the Constitution
of the United States.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:10.010
We would be delinquent in our
duties as members of Congress

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:15.010
if we did not overturn what
the president is proposing.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:17.640
That speech worked because the
resolution passed the House

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.830
with 245 votes, including 13 Republicans

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:23.040
who sided with their
Democratic colleagues.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.940
And that was when things
got really interesting.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.090
See, because this is what's
called a privileged resolution,

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:32.220
it means that the Senate
can't just ignore it.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.053
Why?

00:04:33.053 --> 00:04:33.886
Don't ask why.

00:04:33.886 --> 00:04:35.120
The rules of Congress
are harder to understand

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.820
than Rob Kardashian's fame.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.190
Actually, probably not that
hard, but still difficult.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.530
Which puts Senator Majority
Leader Mitch McConnell

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:44.870
in a major pickle, and yes
that is a technical term.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.450
Because if 51 senators
vote for the resolution,

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:51.260
then it goes to President
Donald Trump's desk,

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:54.590
and that appears to be exactly
what is going to happen.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:56.830
Democrats hold 47 seats in the Senate.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.470
All of them are expected
to vote for the resolution,

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:00.650
so that's 47.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.460
And over the past few weeks, a quartet,

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.630
47 plus four equals 51,
of Republican senators,

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:08.770
Rand Paul, Lisa Murkowski,
Susan Collins, and Thom Tillis,

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:11.310
have said they will vote
for the resolution too.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.730
And it honestly seems likely

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.070
more Republicans will follow that lead,

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.820
citing concerns that if Trump can declare

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.300
a national emergency
to get his wall built,

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.160
then Democratic presidents
could do the same to,

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.230
say, pay for a plan to,

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.880
I don't know, eliminate climate change.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.610
So, in early-March, McConnell
sounded basically resigned

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.980
to losing this resolution vote.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:34.020
- What is clear in the Senate
is there will be enough votes

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.623
to pass the resolution of disapproval,

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:42.420
which will then be
vetoed by the president,

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:43.820
and then, in all likelihood,

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.480
the veto will be upheld in the House.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:47.950
- And he's right,

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:50.744
and that's the strange
thing about all of this.

00:05:50.744 --> 00:05:53.370
If, and, really, if
I'm being honest, when,

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.770
the Senate passes the resolution

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:56.650
to end the national emergency,

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:58.410
Trump has already said he will veto it.

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.430
And it would be some history.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.590
It would be the first veto
of Trump's presidency.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.710
So, Trump vetoes it.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:05.980
Then Congress would need two-thirds votes

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:07.810
in the House and the Senate

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:10.320
to overturn that presidential veto,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.820
and that is just not happening.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:14.320
The votes just aren't there.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:15.690
They're not even really close to there.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.060
So, Trump will get the money for his wall.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.130
But Congress will have
made clear in the process

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.630
that they don't like it

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.640
and they think it opens Pandora's box

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.180
for every future president,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:30.800
and they will be exactly 100% correct.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.240
And that is the Po!nt.

